Exactly. The Golden Thread needs to preserve which version I read, how it was expressed, and what changed when I translated it. The rungs can carry that lineage.

* Version control pins each source to a commit, path, and content hash. If a passage changes, we can trace which translations were made from the earlier version.
* Formatting preserves the original bytes and lets a translation point to the exact passage it used. Line breaks, indentation, emphasis, and even silence can affect the reading.
* Translation gets its own version. Each translation records what it tried to preserve, what it adapted, and where the reading is uncertain. Two readings can branch from the same passage, and a correction can target either one.

Take the slow blink. One rung can carry “I remember” and another “take a beat.” Each can point back to the same gesture and cite the context that supports its reading. Change the timing, and you have a new source version. Change your interpretation, and you have a new translation version.

That gives the wink a precise move. One rung can say, “Same source. Read this part differently.”